    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, '301: During the Dix-Hallpike maneuver to diagnose Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo (BPPV), what is the underlying pathophysiology causing the nystagmus?', '["Viral inflammation of the vestibular nerve", "Meniere''s disease (endolymphatic hydrops)", "Dislodged otoconia (calcium carbonate crystals) floating in the semicircular canals", "Ischemia of the brainstem"]', 2, 'BPPV occurs when otoconia from the utricle dislodge and inappropriately enter the semicircular canals (usually the posterior canal), causing abnormal fluid movement and vertigo when the head changes position.'),
    (v_topic_id, '302: What is the primary difference between a TIA (Transient Ischemic Attack) and a Stroke (CVA)?', '["A TIA only involves the anterior circulation", "A TIA involves no permanent tissue infarction and symptoms resolve completely within 24 hours", "A TIA is caused by hemorrhage, while a CVA is ischemic", "A TIA only affects the cranial nerves"]', 1, 'By classical definition, a TIA is a transient episode of neurologic dysfunction without acute infarction, resolving within 24 hours.'),
    (v_topic_id, '303: Which physical exam test is highly sensitive for detecting a subtle upper motor neuron lesion in the upper extremity?', '["Romberg test", "Pronator drift test", "Tinel''s sign", "Phalen''s test"]', 1, 'Pronator drift is extremely sensitive for subtle contralateral upper motor neuron damage, as the weaker supinator muscles are overcome by the pronators when visual feedback is removed.'),
    (v_topic_id, '304: A patient has lost pain and temperature sensation on the RIGHT side of the face and the LEFT side of the body. They also have ataxia and Horner syndrome on the right. Diagnosis?', '["Lateral medullary syndrome (Wallenberg syndrome)", "Medial medullary syndrome", "Weber syndrome (Midbrain stroke)", "Locked-in syndrome"]', 0, 'Wallenberg syndrome (due to PICA occlusion) classically causes ipsilateral facial sensory loss and contralateral body sensory loss, along with vestibular and cerebellar signs.'),
    (v_topic_id, '305: A 75-year-old patient has resting tremors, a shuffling gait, and "cogwheel" rigidity. What is the pathological hallmark found in the brain of patients with this disease?', '["Neurofibrillary tangles", "Amyloid plaques", "Lewy bodies", "Negri bodies"]', 2, 'Parkinson''s disease is characterized by the loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra and the presence of intracellular Lewy bodies (alpha-synuclein aggregates).'),
    (v_topic_id, '306: Which deep tendon reflex corresponds to the L4 nerve root?', '["Biceps reflex", "Triceps reflex", "Patellar (knee) reflex", "Achilles (ankle) reflex"]', 2, 'The patellar reflex tests the L3-L4 nerve roots.'),
    (v_topic_id, '307: Which deep tendon reflex corresponds to the S1 nerve root?', '["Biceps reflex", "Triceps reflex", "Patellar reflex", "Achilles reflex"]', 3, 'The Achilles reflex tests the S1 nerve root.'),
    (v_topic_id, '308: A patient is unable to stand on their tiptoes. This indicates weakness of the gastrocnemius/soleus complex, primarily innervated by which nerve root?', '["L3", "L4", "L5", "S1"]', 3, 'S1 provides motor innervation to the plantarflexors of the foot.'),
    (v_topic_id, '309: A patient is unable to walk on their heels (inability to dorsiflex the foot). This "foot drop" primarily indicates a lesion of which nerve root or peripheral nerve?', '["L3", "L4", "L5 (or common peroneal nerve)", "S1"]', 2, 'The L5 nerve root (and the common peroneal/fibular nerve) provides motor innervation to the tibialis anterior, which dorsiflexes the foot.'),
    (v_topic_id, '310: "Clasp-knife" spasticity (initial resistance to passive movement followed by a sudden release) is characteristic of:', '["Parkinson''s disease", "Cerebellar lesions", "Lower motor neuron lesions", "Upper motor neuron lesions"]', 3, 'Clasp-knife spasticity is a classic sign of UMN (corticospinal tract) damage, unlike the "lead-pipe" or "cogwheel" rigidity seen in extrapyramidal (Parkinsonian) disease.'),
    (v_topic_id, '311: "Cogwheel" rigidity (a ratchety, stop-and-go resistance to passive movement) is a hallmark of:', '["Upper motor neuron lesions", "Lower motor neuron lesions", "Basal ganglia (extrapyramidal) disorders, such as Parkinson''s", "Cerebellar ataxia"]', 2, 'Cogwheel rigidity results from the combination of lead-pipe rigidity and a superimposed resting tremor, pathognomonic for Parkinsonism.'),
    (v_topic_id, '312: When evaluating the sensory system, a tuning fork is applied to a bony prominence. Which frequency of tuning fork is traditionally used to test vibration sense?', '["128 Hz", "256 Hz", "512 Hz", "1024 Hz"]', 0, 'A 128 Hz tuning fork is used for testing vibration sense (dorsal columns). A 512 Hz tuning fork is used for testing hearing (Weber/Rinne).'),
    (v_topic_id, '313: A patient exhibits an involuntary, arrhythmic, "dancing" or "writhing" movement of the limbs and face. This movement disorder is called:', '["Chorea", "Myoclonus", "Essential tremor", "Asterixis"]', 0, 'Chorea (as seen in Huntington''s disease or Sydenham''s chorea) involves rapid, irregular, non-stereotyped, involuntary movements.'),
    (v_topic_id, '314: Hemiballismus is characterized by violent, flinging movements of one side of the body. It is classically caused by a stroke involving the:', '["Substantia nigra", "Subthalamic nucleus", "Caudate nucleus", "Globus pallidus"]', 1, 'The subthalamic nucleus normally inhibits the thalamus. Damage to it (e.g., from a lacunar stroke) causes unchecked thalamic output, leading to violent flinging movements on the contralateral side.'),
    (v_topic_id, '315: The MMSE (Mini-Mental State Examination) is out of 30 points. A score below what threshold generally suggests significant cognitive impairment or dementia?', '["28", "24", "20", "15"]', 1, 'A score of less than 24 on the MMSE is generally considered indicative of cognitive impairment, though it must be adjusted for education level.'),
    (v_topic_id, '316: Which of the following is a key feature that distinguishes delirium from dementia?', '["Delirium is characterized by an acute onset and fluctuating level of consciousness", "Dementia has an acute onset", "Delirium only affects memory, sparing attention", "Dementia primarily affects young adults"]', 0, 'Delirium is an acute, fluctuating confusional state often triggered by infection or drugs, characterized prominently by inattention. Dementia is a chronic, progressive decline.'),
    (v_topic_id, '317: A patient cannot recognize familiar faces, even though their vision is perfectly normal. This specific type of agnosia is called:', '["Anosognosia", "Prosopagnosia", "Astereognosis", "Alexia"]', 1, 'Prosopagnosia (face blindness) is caused by bilateral lesions to the fusiform gyrus in the temporal/occipital lobes.'),
    (v_topic_id, '318: A patient who denies their own illness or paralyzed limb (e.g., after a severe right hemisphere stroke) is exhibiting:', '["Prosopagnosia", "Anosognosia", "Aphasia", "Apraxia"]', 1, 'Anosognosia is a profound denial or unawareness of one''s own neurological deficit, commonly seen with right parietal lobe lesions.'),
    (v_topic_id, '319: The inability to carry out a learned, purposeful motor act despite intact motor and sensory systems (e.g., unable to demonstrate how to use a comb) is called:', '["Aphasia", "Agnosia", "Apraxia", "Ataxia"]', 2, 'Apraxia is a disorder of motor planning, usually due to a lesion in the premotor or parietal cortex of the dominant hemisphere.'),
    (v_topic_id, '320: Which of the following physical exam findings is the most sensitive indicator of early compartment syndrome?', '["Absent pulses", "Pallor of the limb", "Pain out of proportion to the injury, especially with passive stretch of the muscles", "Paresis (weakness) of the limb"]', 2, 'Pain out of proportion to the injury, particularly on passive stretch of the involved compartment''s muscles, is the earliest and most reliable sign. Pulselessness is a very late and dire sign.'),
    (v_topic_id, '321: A patient has "saddle anesthesia," loss of bowel/bladder control, and bilateral leg weakness after lifting a heavy box. This constitutes a neurosurgical emergency known as:', '["Spinal stenosis", "Cauda equina syndrome", "Guillain-Barre syndrome", "Multiple sclerosis"]', 1, 'Cauda equina syndrome requires immediate MRI and surgical decompression to prevent permanent paralysis and incontinence.'),
    (v_topic_id, '322: The "Spurling test" (neck extension, lateral bending, and downward compression) is used to evaluate for:', '["Meningitis", "Cervical radiculopathy (nerve root compression)", "Cervical myelopathy", "Thoracic outlet syndrome"]', 1, 'Spurling''s maneuver narrows the neural foramen. If a nerve root is inflamed or compressed, this reproduces the radicular pain radiating down the arm.'),
    (v_topic_id, '323: A patient reports pain radiating down the posterior aspect of their thigh and calf to the bottom of their foot. The straight leg raise test is positive. This is classic "sciatica." Which nerve root is most commonly involved?', '["L2 or L3", "L3 or L4", "L5 or S1", "S2 or S3"]', 2, 'The sciatic nerve is composed of roots L4-S3. Herniated discs at L4-L5 (compressing L5) or L5-S1 (compressing S1) are the most common causes of sciatica.'),
    (v_topic_id, '324: A child complains of knee pain, but physical exam of the knee is completely normal. The examiner should ALWAYS also evaluate the:', '["Ankle joint", "Hip joint", "Lumbar spine", "Foot arches"]', 1, 'Hip pathology in children (like Legg-Calve-Perthes disease or Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis) frequently refers pain to the ipsilateral knee via the obturator nerve.'),
    (v_topic_id, '325: A newborn boy is noted to have a palpable clunk when the hip is gently adducted and pushed posteriorly (Barlow maneuver). What is the appropriate management?', '["Immediate open reduction surgery", "Application of a Pavlik harness to keep the hip flexed and abducted", "Observation; it resolves spontaneously in all cases", "Triple diapering"]', 1, 'Developmental dysplasia of the hip (DDH) requires early intervention. The Pavlik harness maintains the hip in flexion and abduction, stabilizing the joint while the acetabulum develops.'),
    (v_topic_id, '326: A patient has swelling, erythema, and severe pain in the first MTP joint. Arthrocentesis reveals needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals. The diagnosis is:', '["Pseudogout", "Rheumatoid arthritis", "Septic arthritis", "Gout"]', 3, 'Gout is caused by monosodium urate crystals, which are needle-shaped and negatively birefringent under polarized light. Pseudogout crystals (CPPD) are rhomboid and weakly positively birefringent.'),
    (v_topic_id, '327: A patient has "sausage digits" (dactylitis), silvery-scaled plaques on their elbows, and pitting of their fingernails. Which joint is typically affected in this arthritis?', '["Distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints", "Proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joints only", "Metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joints only", "Wrist joints only"]', 0, 'Psoriatic arthritis classically involves the DIP joints, unlike rheumatoid arthritis which typically spares the DIP joints.'),
    (v_topic_id, '328: Ankylosing spondylitis primarily affects which area of the musculoskeletal system?', '["Small joints of the hands", "Knees and ankles", "Sacroiliac (SI) joints and the spine", "Shoulders and elbows"]', 2, 'Ankylosing spondylitis is an inflammatory arthritis that begins in the SI joints (sacroiliitis) and progresses up the spine, eventually leading to fusion ("bamboo spine").'),
    (v_topic_id, '329: A 10-year-old boy presents with right knee pain and a visible bump just below the patella. The tibial tuberosity is exquisitely tender. Diagnosis?', '["Osgood-Schlatter disease", "Patellar tendinitis", "Meniscal tear", "Osteosarcoma"]', 0, 'Osgood-Schlatter disease is an apophysitis of the tibial tuberosity, very common in growing adolescents active in jumping/running sports.'),
    (v_topic_id, '330: In a patient with osteoarthritis of the knee, which compartment is most commonly and severely affected first?', '["Medial tibiofemoral compartment", "Lateral tibiofemoral compartment", "Patellofemoral compartment", "All compartments are affected equally and simultaneously"]', 0, 'The medial compartment bears the majority of the body''s weight during the gait cycle, making it the most common site for early OA, often leading to a varus ("bow-legged") deformity.'),
    (v_topic_id, '331: During the physical exam of a patient with suspected rheumatoid arthritis, the examiner squeezes the MCP joints across the hand. If this causes significant pain, it is a positive:', '["Squeeze test (indicating synovitis)", "Phalen test", "Finkelstein test", "Tinel test"]', 0, 'The "squeeze test" across the MCPs or MTPs is a simple, sensitive bedside indicator of active synovitis in early inflammatory arthritis.'),
    (v_topic_id, '332: What is the defining characteristic of a 3rd-degree (complete) muscle strain or ligament tear?', '["Microscopic tearing with intact strength", "Partial tearing with pain and reduced strength", "Complete rupture of the muscle/ligament, leading to joint instability and often less pain than a partial tear", "Inflammation of the tendon sheath without tearing"]', 2, 'A complete tear often presents with massive swelling, profound weakness, and gross instability. Paradoxically, once completely ruptured, stretching the unit no longer causes tension, so pain may actually decrease compared to a partial tear.'),
    (v_topic_id, '333: A patient has a "winging" of the scapula when asked to push against a wall. This indicates paralysis of the serratus anterior muscle, innervated by the:', '["Long thoracic nerve", "Spinal accessory nerve", "Dorsal scapular nerve", "Thoracodorsal nerve"]', 0, 'The long thoracic nerve (C5-C7) innervates the serratus anterior. It is classically injured during axillary node dissection in breast surgery or trauma to the lateral chest wall.'),
    (v_topic_id, '334: The "drawer test" of the shoulder (anterior/posterior load and shift) evaluates the stability of the:', '["Acromioclavicular joint", "Sternoclavicular joint", "Glenohumeral joint", "Scapulothoracic articulation"]', 2, 'The load and shift test evaluates the laxity of the glenohumeral capsule and ligaments, checking for anterior or posterior instability.'),
    (v_topic_id, '335: A patient has a painful, stiff shoulder that has lost both active AND passive range of motion in all directions. X-rays are normal. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Rotator cuff tear", "Adhesive capsulitis (frozen shoulder)", "Shoulder impingement", "Glenohumeral osteoarthritis"]', 1, 'Adhesive capsulitis is characterized by a global loss of both active and passive ROM due to severe fibrosis of the joint capsule. Rotator cuff tears typically retain full passive ROM.'),
    (v_topic_id, '336: A patient with a humerus mid-shaft fracture is unable to extend their wrist or fingers. Which nerve was injured?', '["Median nerve", "Ulnar nerve", "Musculocutaneous nerve", "Radial nerve"]', 3, 'The radial nerve wraps tightly around the posterior humerus in the spiral groove. A mid-shaft fracture can easily sever or stretch it, causing "wrist drop."'),
    (v_topic_id, '337: A patient with a supracondylar fracture of the humerus (common in children) is at high risk for injuring which artery, potentially leading to Volkmann''s ischemic contracture?', '["Radial artery", "Ulnar artery", "Brachial artery", "Axillary artery"]', 2, 'The brachial artery runs directly over the distal humerus and can be entrapped or lacerated by the displaced fracture fragments.'),
    (v_topic_id, '338: A patient with a fracture of the surgical neck of the humerus is at risk for injuring the axillary nerve. How would this present on physical exam?', '["Inability to extend the wrist", "Numbness over the lateral shoulder (regimental badge area) and weakness in arm abduction", "Claw hand deformity", "Loss of biceps reflex"]', 1, 'The axillary nerve innervates the deltoid and teres minor and supplies sensation to the skin overlying the deltoid.'),
    (v_topic_id, '339: A colles fracture is a fracture of the distal radius with dorsal displacement of the distal fragment, giving the wrist a characteristic shape. What is this classic deformity called?', '["Swan neck deformity", "Boutonniere deformity", "Dinner fork deformity", "Mallet finger deformity"]', 2, 'The dorsal displacement and angulation of the distal radius create a bump that resembles the curve of a dinner fork.'),
    (v_topic_id, '340: A patient has "mallet finger," which is the inability to actively extend the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joint. What is the mechanism of this injury?', '["Rupture of the flexor digitorum profundus tendon", "Avulsion or rupture of the terminal extensor tendon at its insertion on the distal phalanx", "Dislocation of the PIP joint", "Rupture of the central slip of the extensor tendon"]', 1, 'Mallet finger occurs when a forced flexion force (like a basketball hitting the fingertip) avulses the extensor tendon, leaving the DIP joint stuck in flexion.'),
    (v_topic_id, '341: A patient cannot actively flex the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joint of their ring finger after catching it on a player''s jersey during a football game. This injury is called:', '["Mallet finger", "Jersey finger", "Trigger finger", "Boutonniere deformity"]', 1, 'Jersey finger is the avulsion of the flexor digitorum profundus (FDP) tendon from the distal phalanx, resulting in the inability to flex the DIP joint.'),
    (v_topic_id, '342: To test the L5 nerve root during a physical exam, the examiner should ask the patient to:', '["Walk on their toes", "Extend their knee against resistance", "Extend their great toe against resistance", "Flex their hip against resistance"]', 2, 'Extensor hallucis longus (EHL) is innervated primarily by L5. Resisted great toe extension is a very specific test for L5 radiculopathy.'),
    (v_topic_id, '343: A patient has a positive Apley grind test (pain with compression and rotation of the tibia while the patient is prone with the knee flexed to 90 degrees). This points to an injury of the:', '["ACL", "PCL", "Meniscus", "Patellar tendon"]', 2, 'The Apley grind test grinds the menisci between the femur and tibia. Pain or clicking indicates a meniscal tear.'),
    (v_topic_id, '344: A patient has a "Baker''s cyst." Where is this located?', '["Anterior knee, over the patella", "Posterior knee, in the popliteal fossa", "Lateral knee, over the fibular head", "Medial knee, over the pes anserinus"]', 1, 'A Baker''s (popliteal) cyst is a fluid-filled distention of the gastrocnemius-semimembranosus bursa, usually secondary to underlying intra-articular pathology like OA or a meniscal tear.'),
    (v_topic_id, '345: The "Thompson test" is performed by squeezing the calf of a prone patient. What is the NORMAL response?', '["The toes dorsiflex", "The foot plantarflexes", "The knee flexes", "The foot does not move"]', 1, 'Squeezing the calf physically pulls the intact Achilles tendon, causing passive plantarflexion. If the tendon is completely ruptured, the foot will not move (a positive Thompson test).'),
    (v_topic_id, '346: A child presents with "nursemaid''s elbow." What is the anatomical injury?', '["Fracture of the radial head", "Subluxation of the radial head from beneath the annular ligament", "Dislocation of the humeroulnar joint", "Avulsion of the medial epicondyle"]', 1, 'Nursemaid''s elbow occurs when a sudden longitudinal pull on the arm of a toddler causes the radial head to slip partially out of the annular ligament. It is reduced by supination and flexion.'),
    (v_topic_id, '347: A patient has severe pain in the anatomical snuffbox after falling on an outstretched hand (FOOSH). X-rays are initially negative. How should this be managed?', '["Prescribe NSAIDs and advise normal use", "Place in an ace bandage and follow up as needed", "Assume a scaphoid fracture, place in a thumb spica splint, and repeat imaging in 1-2 weeks", "Perform an immediate carpal tunnel release"]', 2, 'Scaphoid fractures often do not appear on initial X-rays. Because of the high risk of avascular necrosis, any snuffbox tenderness after a FOOSH must be treated as a fracture until proven otherwise.'),
    (v_topic_id, '348: A "Bouchard''s node" is a bony enlargement located at which joint?', '["The DIP joint", "The PIP joint", "The MCP joint", "The wrist"]', 1, 'Bouchard''s nodes are at the Proximal Interphalangeal (PIP) joints, and Heberden''s nodes are at the Distal Interphalangeal (DIP) joints, both signs of osteoarthritis.'),
    (v_topic_id, '349: A patient presents with generalized aching, severe fatigue, and widespread tenderness in specific trigger points (e.g., neck, shoulders, hips), but all laboratory tests and joint exams are entirely normal. This is characteristic of:', '["Rheumatoid arthritis", "Polymyalgia rheumatica", "Fibromyalgia", "Osteoarthritis"]', 2, 'Fibromyalgia is a chronic pain syndrome characterized by widespread musculoskeletal pain, fatigue, and specific tender points without underlying joint inflammation or abnormal labs.'),
    (v_topic_id, '350: A patient over 50 presents with sudden, severe aching and morning stiffness in the shoulders, neck, and pelvic girdle, accompanied by an extremely elevated ESR, but normal strength on exam. This is the classic presentation of:', '["Fibromyalgia", "Polymyalgia rheumatica (PMR)", "Polymyositis", "Systemic lupus erythematosus"]', 1, 'PMR classically presents with severe proximal muscle stiffness (not weakness) and a highly elevated ESR, and is frequently associated with Giant Cell Arteritis.');
